{
  "title": "US Policymakers' Views on AI Governance and Regulation",
  "authors": [
    "Sophia Hatz",
    "Noemi Dreksler",
    "Kevin Wei",
    "Baobao Zhang"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "arxivId": "2501.09606",
  "abstract": "This paper presents a survey of local US policymakers' views on the future impact and regulation of AI. The survey reveals a mix of concern, optimism, and uncertainty about AI's impacts, with a majority of policymakers supporting government regulation and oversight. The findings highlight the need for capacity-building initiatives and bipartisan coordination to mitigate policy fragmentation and build a cohesive framework for AI governance in the US.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The rapid advancement of AI has led to increased concerns about its societal implications and regulatory needs. <strong>Local policymakers</strong> play a crucial role in shaping state and federal AI legislation, but their views on AI governance and regulation are not well understood. This paper aims to fill this knowledge gap by surveying local US policymakers about their expectations of AI's future impact and their attitudes towards specific regulatory policies.",
